fund-csharp/25-background-work-and-grace.explanation:
Wait on the reader, catch per job, and let the stopping token decide what a cancellation means:

```csharp
while (!stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested && await jobs.WaitToReadAsync(stoppingToken))
    while (!stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested && jobs.TryRead(out var job))
    {
        try { await handle(job, stoppingToken); done++; log.Add($"done {job.Id}"); }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested) { log.Add($"stopped {job.Id}"); }
        catch (Exception e) { failed++; log.Add($"failed {job.Id}: {e.Message}"); }
    }
// around the loops: catch (OperationCanceledException) when (stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested) { }
```

The tests catch the usual wrong loops. Without the per-job catch, the `card declined` exception leaves the worker and "a job that throws is logged, and the next job still runs" fails. Filtering on the exception type, `when (e is not OperationCanceledException)`, lets the job's own timeout escape instead of counting it as failed. Dropping the stop check from the inner loop starts `j2` and `j3` after a stop. Draining with `TryRead` and returning instead of waiting ends the idle worker before the late job arrives. Letting the cancelled wait throw breaks the quiet stop of the idle worker.

