fund-csharp/25-background-work-and-grace.explanation:
Complete the writer, then read with `TryRead` so the deadline decides each job's fate rather than ending the loop:

```csharp
queue.Writer.TryComplete();
while (queue.Reader.TryRead(out var job))
{
    if (deadline.IsCancellationRequested) { handBack(job); handedBack++; continue; }
    try { await handle(job, deadline); done++; }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (deadline.IsCancellationRequested) { handBack(job); handedBack++; }
    catch (Exception) { failed++; }
}
```

A completed channel still gives up the items it holds, so nothing is lost by completing first. Wrong versions the tests catch: skipping `TryComplete` accepts the late job in "stopping refuses new work before the first queued job runs"; a `break` at the deadline instead of handing back loses `j3` and `j4`; counting the deadline's cancellation as a failure reports `j2` as failed instead of handed back; no deadline check before starting starts `j2` and `j3` after the deadline; and `await foreach` over `ReadAllAsync(deadline)` throws as soon as the deadline fires instead of handing the rest back.
